130
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:41,920
In the 16th century, Spanish conquistadors

131
00:06:41,921 --> 00:06:44,080
brought potatoes to Europe from Peru.

132
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:46,920
But for 200 years, nobody would eat them.

133
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:49,160
People actually thought they were poisonous

134
00:06:49,161 --> 00:06:51,420
or only worthy to feed livestock.

135
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:54,200
That is, until a French pharmacist

136
00:06:54,201 --> 00:06:56,200
named Antoine Parmentier

137
00:06:56,201 --> 00:06:59,300
saw the potato as something that could feed a nation.

138
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,040
And just like we would today,

139
00:07:01,041 --> 00:07:04,800
he turned to the ultimate influencer of public opinion.

140
00:07:04,801 --> 00:07:09,800
He got King Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette to wear potato flowers on their clothes

141
00:07:09,801 --> 00:07:13,140
as a way of enticing people to see potatoes in a new way.

142
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:15,840
And starting with that royal endorsement,

143
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:19,400
potatoes soon became one of the top food sources for Europe,

144
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,580
then America, and then the rest of the world.

145
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:27,840
There are a few different accounts of how the potato went from feeding European nations

146
00:07:27,841 --> 00:07:30,160
