00:26:39,541 --> 00:26:42,580
that the movie theater owners could actually make more money

510
00:26:42,581 --> 00:26:44,180
off the popcorn they would sell to their patrons

511
00:26:44,181 --> 00:26:45,520
and off the tickets to their movies.

512
00:26:46,060 --> 00:26:52,060
So theaters really fully embraced popcorn with Gerard W. Dixon in 1938,

513
00:26:52,061 --> 00:26:56,060
when he actually installed popcorn poppers in the front of his movie theater.

514
00:26:56,061 --> 00:26:58,060
And so that meant that when patrons came in,

515
00:26:58,061 --> 00:27:01,060
they were sort of accosted by the smell of hot oil and butter

516
00:27:01,061 --> 00:27:05,060
that made them want to have to get popcorn before they went in to watch the movie.

517
00:27:05,061 --> 00:27:11,060
Let's all go to the lobby. Let's all go to the lobby.

518
00:27:11,061 --> 00:27:15,060
Let's all go to the lobby to get ourselves a treat.

519
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:19,060
So there was a period when popcorn sales started to decrease,

520
00:27:19,140 --> 00:27:21,060
and this was in the 1970s and 1980s,

521
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:23,700
when viewership went from being sort of a communal,

522
00:27:23,780 --> 00:27:25,300
going to the movie theaters affair,

523
00:27:25,380 --> 00:27:27,580
to staying at home and watching on TV.

524
00:27:27,660 --> 00:27:30,900
By the 1980s, two inventions went mainstream

525
00:27:30,980 --> 00:27:32,700
into every house in America

526
00:27:32,780 --> 00:27:36,420
and kept popcorn as part of the movie-watching experience.

527
00:27:36,500 --> 00:27:40,260
By the 1980s, America's love affair

528
00:27:40,340 --> 00:27:43,420
with mass-marketed popular culture was at an all-time high.

529
00:27:43,500 --> 00:27:44,940
We even had PCRs.

530
00:27:44,941 --> 00:27:47,480
Films you could see at the movie theater,

531
00:27:47,481 --> 00:27:53,040
you could then see six feet in front of you on a television.

532
00:27:53,041 --> 00:27:55,780
And of course, microwave popcorn.

533
00:27:55,781 --> 00:27:57,580
Makes all the sense in the world.

534
00:27:57,581 --> 00:28:00,420
In the 1940s, a Raytheon scientist

535
00:28:00,421 --> 00:28:02,500
was experimenting with a magnetron

536
00:28:02,501 --> 00:28:06,920
when he felt a candy bar in his pocket melt by the microwaves.

537
00:28:06,921 --> 00:28:10,140
He got the idea to expose popcorn to the microwaves,

538
00:28:10,141 --> 00:28:13,460
and boom, it immediately popped all over the lab,

539
00:28:13,461 --> 00:28:15,620
And the rest is history.

540
00:28:15,621 --> 00:28:18,800
Popping popcorn was even included in the original patent

541
00:28:18,801 --> 00:28:20,640
for the microwave oven.

542
00:28:20,641 --> 00:28:23,240
Since then, manufacturers continue

543
00:28:23,241 --> 00:28:27,240
to make new flavors and products to keep the popcorn industry

